title: Comparisons
tags: Query
kind: reference
summary: Comparisons are same-type, numbers form one domain, dates compare by instant; an operand of another type, comparing with nothing, or ordering a type with no order is refused up front.

A field name has one value type in the repository ([[One value type per field name]]),
so a comparison is always ''same-type'': the operand is compared with rows of the
field's type, with no cross-type rules. `int` and `float` form one numeric domain;
`datetime` compares by its instant (Unix milliseconds). A `nothing` row never matches
a value comparison ([[Presence and absence]]).

A date operand needs the date literal — `mfr_mtime > @"2024-01-01"` or
`@1704067200000`; a plain string is refused, the field being a `datetime`. The ISO-8601 forms are those `core::date::iso_to_ms` accepts
(`@"2024-01-01"`, `@"2024-01-01 12:30:00"`), read as UTC; an unparsable one is
refused.

On a `tree_ref` field a string operand names a node: `mfr_path = "/music/jazz"` is
the one node at that path ([[Field aspects]]).

!! Refused before evaluation

Two shapes have no useful meaning and are refused with `400` before anything
evaluates the query — a property of the query, not of what serves it:

* a comparison whose operand is `nothing` — absence is asked with `IS ABSENT` / `IS UNKNOWN`;
* an ''ordered'' comparison (`<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`) on a type with no meaningful order: `bool` and the reference types. Equality on them is fine; strings order lexicographically, numbers and dates numerically.

Other refusals need the field's stored type, and are made one step later from the
daemon's field catalogue, still before evaluation:

* ''an operand of another type than the field's'' (`=`, `!=`, `in` and the ordered operators) — `tag = 2024` on a `string` field, `mfr_mtime > "2024"` on a `datetime`. It could only ever match nothing, or under `!=` every holder, so it is the mistake it almost always is; the message says how to write the operand (`"2024"`, `@"2024-01-01"`). `int` and `float` are one domain. On a `tree_ref` or a `hierarchy_ref`, a string names a node or, under `:value`, `:parent` and `:path`, a name or a path; a value of the field's own type is the node itself. A field with no data has no type to hold the operand to: the query is accepted and matches nothing. The simplified language types a bare number as a number (`tag=2024` expands to `tag = 2024`), so it is refused the same way (user decision, 9 November 2026);
* a tree-only aspect on another type;
* `osm` in path mode on a non-`tree_ref`;
* a `:path` operand that is not a string.

Size limits are checked at the same point ([[Query limits]]).
